#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# max

#% text
# `max(a, b, ...)` gives the largest of the real numbers `a`, `b`, ....

#% text
# `max(xs)` gives the largest element of the list `xs`; nested lists are searched through.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact, rational, machine and ball numbers are compared by value; the result keeps its kind.

#% item
# Closed numeric expressions such as `pi` and `sqrt(2)` are not compared: the call stays (apply `n` first).

#% item
# With symbolic arguments the call stays, keeping only the arguments that may still be the largest.

#% item
# `max([])` is a failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
max(3, 7, 2)
#% output
#= 7

#% cell
max([4, 1, 9, 2])
#% output
#= 9

#% cell
max([[1, 5], [3, 2]])
#% output
#= 5

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Numbers of different kinds:

#% cell
max(1/3, 0.3, 2/7)
#% output
#= 1/3

#% text
# Symbols:

#% cell
max(x, 2, 5)
#% output
#= max(x, 2, 5)

#% cell
max([])
#% output
#! failure(argument, max, 1)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A value kept away from zero before a logarithm, as in the wall-profile script:

#% cell
fn safe_log(x) = log(max(x, 1.0e-9))
[safe_log(2.0), safe_log(0.0)]
#% output
#= [0.6931471805599453, -20.72326583694641]

#% text
# The range of a data set:

#% cell
data = [2.3, -0.7, 4.1, 1.8]
max(data) - min(data)
#% output
#= 4.8

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `clamp` limits a value from both sides with `min` and `max`:

#% cell
clamp(12, 0, 10)
#% output
#= 10

#% text
# `max_by` compares by a key and returns the element:

#% cell
max_by(["kiwi", "banana", "fig"], string_length)
#% output
#= "banana"

#% text
# The position of the maximum:

#% cell
v = [3, 8, 1]
position(v, max(v))
#% output
#= [[2]]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Strings are not numbers and are not compared:

#% cell
max("a", "b")
#% output
#= max("a", "b")

#% text
# Closed numeric expressions stay unevaluated; their numerical values compare:

#% cell
max(pi, 3.2, sqrt(10))
#% output
#= 3.2

#% cell
max(n([pi, 3.2, sqrt(10)]))
#% output
#= 3.2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `min`, `max_by`, `clamp`, `sort`, `find_maximum`
